  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving access to essential and life-saving health care services, through supporting WASH in health facilities and vector control interventions for flood-affected population in 31 localities in 9 states</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The main objective of this $1.3 million WHO project is to improve access to essential and life-saving health services to reduce flood-related morbidity and mortality. To that end, WHO will support the prevention and mitigation of waterborne and vector diseases through dissemination of risk communication messages, strengthen disease surveillance and response systems by conducting refresher trainings for health workers as well as operational support for Rapid Response Teams, and procure essential supplies for the timely identification of potential disease outbreaks, among other activities. In total, WHO will provide health support to 100,000 people, including 19,600 women, 29,401 girls, and 10,000 persons living with disabilities.</narrative>
  </description>
